","Here how to calculate the magnetic field at the center of a circular coil:

**Formula:**

The magnetic field (B) at the center of a circular loop is given by:

B = (μ₀ * N * I) / (2πR)

Where:

*   B is the magnetic field in Tesla (N/m⋅C)
* μ₀ is the permeability of free medium (approximately 4ππ/μ₀₀  = 4π^(-8) N⋅C²/T)
*   n is the number of turns in the loop (in this case, 100)
*   I is the current in the loop (in Amperes)
*   R is the radius of the loop (in meters)

**Values:**

*   n = 100
*   I = 1 A
*   R = 0.2 m
*   μ₀ = 4π^(-8) N⋅C²/T

**Calculation:**

B = (4π^(-8) N⋅C²/T * 100 * 1 A) / (2π * 0.2 m)
B = (4π^(-8) * 100) / (0.4)  T
B = (4π^(-8) * 250) T
B ≈ 1.55 x 10^(-5) T

**Answer:**

The magnetic field at the center of the loop is approximately 1.55 x 10^(-5) Tesla.",ground_truth_similarity,"# Task Description
